Yes. Building a .NET 8 WPF application on Xubuntu is possible, but there are some important caveats:

WPF is Windows-only.
The .NET SDK on Linux cannot build WPF projects natively because WPF depends on Windows desktop targeting packs.
The usual workaround is to run the Windows .NET SDK inside Wine and build the project there.
The resulting executable is still a Windows application.

If your goal is:

Develop on Xubuntu and produce Windows binaries → Wine + Windows .NET SDK works.
Run the WPF application on Linux → WPF itself is not supported. You would need Wine to run it, or migrate to something like Avalonia.
Option 1: Build using Wine (recommended for WPF)
Step 1: Install Wine

If a Snap version is available on your Xubuntu release:

sudo snap install wine-platform-runtime
sudo snap install wine-platform-6-stable

However, Wine Snap support is inconsistent across Ubuntu releases.

The more reliable method is usually:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install wine64 winbind

Verify:

wine --version
Step 2: Create a clean Wine prefix
export WINEPREFIX=$HOME/.wine-dotnet8
wineboot

This creates a dedicated Windows environment.

Step 3: Install Windows .NET 8 SDK

Download the Windows x64 SDK installer:

mkdir ~/downloads
cd ~/downloads

Get the SDK from Microsoft's official site:

Microsoft .NET Downloads

Run:

wine dotnet-sdk-8.0.xxx-win-x64.exe

Follow the installer wizard.

Step 4: Verify .NET inside Wine

Locate the installed SDK:

wine cmd

Inside cmd:

dotnet --info

You should see the SDK information.

Exit:

exit
Step 5: Install Visual Studio Build Tools (optional but often necessary)

Many WPF projects require MSBuild components not present in the standalone SDK.

Download:

Visual Studio Build Tools

Run:

wine vs_BuildTools.exe

Select:

MSBuild
.NET Desktop Build Tools
.NET 8 SDK
Windows SDK

This can take a while.

Step 6: Clone your project
git clone <repo>
cd <repo>
Step 7: Build via Wine

Example:

wine cmd /c dotnet restore

then:

wine cmd /c dotnet build -c Release

or:

wine cmd /c dotnet publish -c Release
Option 2: Use a Windows build container inside Docker

This is generally easier and more reproducible than Wine.

Unfortunately:

Windows containers require a Windows kernel.
Linux Docker hosts cannot run Windows containers directly.

So on Xubuntu this isn't practical unless you're using a remote Windows builder.

Option 3: Use a Windows VM (often the easiest)

Install:

VirtualBox

Create a lightweight Windows 11 VM.

Install:

.NET 8 SDK
Build Tools

Then build via:

dotnet build

This is often more reliable than Wine for WPF.

Typical WPF project file settings

Ensure your .csproj contains something like:

<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <PlatformTarget>AnyCPU</PlatformTarget>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
CI/CD alternative (highly recommended)

Many teams developing on Linux avoid local WPF builds entirely and use:

GitHub Actions
Azure DevOps

with a Windows runner:

runs-on: windows-latest

and:

- uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v4
  with:
    dotnet-version: 8.0.x

- run: dotnet build MyProject.sln -c Release

This is usually the least painful solution because WPF is fundamentally tied to Windows build tooling.

If you share your .csproj and whether you're using packages such as WinForms, WebView2, ClickOnce, COM references, or native DLLs, I can give a more exact Wine setup and point out any parts likely to fail under Wine.
